<h2>Background</h2>
<p>The wife filed a complaint under Section 12 of the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005 along with an application for interim relief under Sections 20 and 23. The Mahila Court awarded ₹10,000 per month in aggregate — ₹2,000 for the wife and ₹8,000 for the minor child living with her — payable from the date of the application. The husband appealed under Section 29, arguing that the wife is educated and earns about ₹50,000 a month from a garment shop, that his own salary of ₹32,400 is consumed substantially by a loan EMI of roughly ₹17,085, and that the child\'s claimed expenses were inflated.</p>

<h2>The three questions the appeal raised</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Was there domestic violence at all?</strong><p>At the interim stage the court needs only a prima facie case. Disputed versions of cruelty and dispossession are matters of trial evidence; the wife\'s allegations crossed the threshold required for interim relief.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Does the wife\'s alleged income defeat the claim?</strong><p>No proof of employment or salary was produced. The court reiterated that the DV Act — unlike the stricter language found elsewhere — does not make inability to maintain oneself a precondition, and that earning capacity cannot be equated with actual earning.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Was the quantum excessive?</strong><p>After deducting only income tax and compulsory contributions — not EMIs or utility bills — the disposable income supported the award under the four-portion apportionment approach. The figure was neither arbitrary nor disproportionate.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The deduction discipline</h2>
<p>The most practically significant part of the judgment is its treatment of liabilities. Husbands resisting maintenance routinely present their net-of-everything income: salary minus EMIs, minus rent, minus insurance premiums, minus credit card dues. The Saket court, following the line of authority in <em>Nitin Sharma</em>, declined to travel that road. Income tax and compulsory statutory deductions such as GPF come off the top; voluntary financial commitments do not. The logic is straightforward — a spouse cannot contract his way out of a statutory maintenance obligation by acquiring debt — and it aligns with the Supreme Court\'s insistence in <em>Rajnesh v. Neha</em> that maintenance be fixed on a realistic, documented picture of means rather than on self-serving arithmetic.</p>

<h2>Reading the award against the formula</h2>
<p>With a disclosed income of about ₹32,400 and no statutory deductions shown beyond the standard ones, the apportionment approach drawn from <em>Annurita Vohra</em> would allocate roughly a quarter of the disposable income to the wife and a quarter to the child. The actual award — ₹2,000 and ₹8,000 — totals less than a third of the disclosed salary, weighted deliberately towards the child. The appellate court found this entirely consistent with the formula, and noted additionally that the household the wife maintains pays no rent, a factor the trial court had legitimately weighed. The appeal was dismissed and the interim order sustained in full, payable until the Section 12 petition itself is decided.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>Interim maintenance under the DV Act and maintenance under Section 125 CrPC — now Section 144 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 — can run in parallel, but amounts paid under one are adjusted against the other. Where both proceedings exist, the financial affidavits filed in each must match; inconsistencies between them are the single most common source of adverse inference in Delhi\'s family litigation.</p>
</div>
